Good question to stop and clarify, because there's an important distinction buried in it.

What I've been doing: testing whether existing, pretrained genomic language models (DNABERT-2 so far; HyenaDNA and Nucleotide Transformer v2 set up but not yet run) can — out of the box, with no training on your task — tell you which 16S region best separates organism X from its near neighbors. The workflow is: feed sequence through the pretrained model → get its embedding → train a cheap classifier (random forest) on top of that embedding → see if it beats simpler, older methods. This is the same research question you're describing ("predict which region to target per organism"), but the thing being tested is whether off-the-shelf gLMs already contain the right signal, not whether a purpose-built/trained one would.

Where the evidence points so far: not well, for the gLM. On the one target where we finally got a legitimate, real-signal test (S. aureus vs. coagulase-negative staph, region V1), a 1990s-era method (count short DNA fragments, train a simple classifier) nearly solved it perfectly (99.7%), while DNABERT-2's embeddings were no better than a coin flip. On the other target tested, neither method found anything (correctly — that organism pair has no real genetic distinction at any region). So far: zero evidence that an existing gLM adds value over classical composition-based methods for this specific task.

This matters for your actual goal. There are really two different projects hiding under "build something that predicts the best region":

1. A recommendation tool that, per organism, tells you which region to sequence — using whichever method wins. If classical k-mer+classifier keeps winning, that tool doesn't need an LLM at all; it's simpler, cheaper, and (so far) more accurate. This is what regioneer (the planned R package) was scoped as.
2. Actually training or fine-tuning a genomic LLM specifically for this discriminability task — a materially bigger undertaking: you'd need a training objective that rewards "separate same-species from near-neighbors," labeled data across many organism panels (not just the 1-2 we've tested), and a lot more compute/engineering. Nothing done so far builds toward this; it would be a new project.

So: are you trying to build (1), a practical region-advisor tool that uses whatever method actually works best, or (2), specifically an LLM-based solution (including possibly fine-tuning one), even if that means more work than the classical approach currently needs?

